## What I polished

**`src/blog/people/drafts/Frank-Lloyd-Wright.md`**

The draft came in already clean on every mechanical gate — 0 contrast pairs, 0 em-dashes, ledgers present. So the real finding was a formula nobody had counted: with the contrast-pair engine gone, the piece had grown a **narrator pointer sentence** in its place. Six of them, four opening paragraphs — "That sentence is the Seven's reframe written in stone," "That line carries the man," "That is the strongest available answer to Wright's critics." Each one steps in front of the evidence and announces what it means, which is the same tell as "not X but Y" wearing a different coat.

I kept the two that land at emotional peaks (Mamah's grave, the Wallace "end of the runway" line) and rewrote the other four so the fact delivers its own meaning. Same treatment on the "here's what everyone misses" opener, which fired five times; cut to three, keeping the cold-open pivot, the grave, and the honest Fallingwater-legend caveat.

**One factual fix:** the 1953 "honest arrogance" quote was described as "twenty-six words." It's 24. Corrected, and the count now leads the sentence instead of trailing it as a flourish.

Other cuts: an invented "three thousand pages"; "a failure of cantilever-comprehension"; a duplicate "volume" metaphor 70 lines apart; a mirror/mirror collision one clause apart in the conclusion; and two back-to-back symmetrical semicolon pairs in the Rabbit Hole Type 5 rebuttal (the contrast engine lint doesn't count). The conclusion still bookends the intro but now advances instead of repeating it near-verbatim.

The full protect list survived — cold open, lieber Meister argument, Okura telegram, curtains-into-handkerchiefs, Philip Johnson concession, final paragraph. The Sullivan section lost its "the casual reading is X / the deeper one is Y" scaffolding, but the argument itself is untouched and hits sooner without it.

Body went 4,488 → 4,447 words, so the pass paid for itself and returned ~41 words of headroom against the 4,500 ceiling.

